4. Qe2
{ White challenges the black queen, and it is Black's queen that stands attacked. Few players have tried this; most develop a piece instead. }
4... f6
{ Black props the queen up with a pawn, but the pin on the file stays. In the one master game that reached this, nobody tried it. }
5. d4
{ White drives a pawn into the centre, and his pawns now hold more of the middle than Black's; a shade ahead as a result. }
*

5... Qxe2+
{ The queens come off and the check comes with it; the king must answer, and the bishop beside him is attacked as well. }
6. Ngxe2
{ White recaptures with the knight; the queens are gone and the middlegame has to be played out. }
6... Na6
{ Black finds a square for the knight on the edge, and the game settles into slower work. }
7. Be3
{ White develops the bishop and keeps a small edge as the play settles. White mates the black king: the attack on the kingside never stopped, and that is what decided the game. }
